==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*m(y)ewh₁-}}
From {{uder|en|frm|mobilité}}, and its source, {{uder|en|la|mobilitas|mōbilitās|mobility}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/mə(ʊ)ˈbɪlɪti/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-mobility.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/moʊˈbɪlɪti/|a=GA}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The ability to [[move]]; capacity for [[movement]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|mobileness}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=15 June 2015|author=w:Hadley Freeman|journal=The Guardian
|passage=I find the enduring existence of high heels both a frustrating mystery and a testament to the triumph of women’s neuroses over their '''mobility'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 December 14|author=David Turner|title=The Edwardian Christmas getaway...|journal=RAIL|issue=972|page=32|text=In the late 19th and early 20th century, the festive season was also a period of great '''mobility''' before, during and after Christmas Day. But the railways kept working.}}
# {{lb|en|now|chiefly|literary}} A tendency to sudden change; [[mutability]], [[changeableness]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} The ability of a military unit to move or be transported to a new position. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|physics}} The degree to which [[particle]]s of a [[liquid]] or [[gas]] are in movement. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|sociology}} The ability of people to move between different social [[level]]s or [[professional]] [[occupation]]s. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-web
|en
|work=New York Times
|date=2020-07-28
|title=Trump Is Trying to Bend Reality to His Will
|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/07/29/opinion/trump-2020-populism.html
|author=Thomas B. Edsall
|passage=The difficulty of rising up the economic ladder is reflected in the decline in '''mobility''' in the United States. {{...}} The frustration over the lack of '''mobility''' is particularly acute for those without college degrees.}}

